WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our next guest is an actress
and Emmy Award-winning writer

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.837
you know from shows
such as "Great News,"

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.605
"The Mindy Project,"
and "30 Rock."

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:09.475
Her new show "Saved by the Bell"
is streaming now on Peacock.

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:10.476
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:11.810
-If we can't agree
at this table,

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.212
then how are we gonna get
the whole school to walk out?

00:00:13.212 --> 00:00:14.880
Bayside has like
a million cliques.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.649
Got the artsy kids,

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.017
the girls who code,

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.752
the cool Christians,

00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:21.187
uncool Christians.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.056
-Mm. Yeah, but there's Douglas
kids in all of those groups.

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:25.358
And you guys are our friends.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:26.759
-Yeah, but being friends with
someone doesn't mean

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.327
you're willing to put your neck
out for them.

00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:30.830
-Listen, I can get people
at this school to do anything.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.766
Did you notice how everyone here
calls soda "fizzy juice?"

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:35.534
I made that happen for a reason
I don't remember.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.971
-Okay, Lexi, you and Jamie
try to get everyone on board.

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:41.174
Devante, Aisha, and I
will reach out to local media

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:42.809
and convince them
to cover the walkout.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.977
-And as soon as my dad picks up,
I'll convince him to come.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:48.948
-Your wait time is approximately
4 billion minutes.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:51.317
-Please welcome to the show
Tracey Wigfield.

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:52.785
How are you, Tracey?

00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:55.288
-Hi, Seth. I'm good.
How are you?

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.556
-I am wonderful.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.657
I know that you are
working on Season 2 of

00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:02.228
"Saved by the Bell" right now
in what is a very packed house.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.363
Who do you have living
under your roof right now?

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:07.633
-Yeah, so we --
it's my husband and I.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:09.668
And my husband is
a comedy writer too.

00:01:09.668 --> 00:01:11.671
And then we have two kids.

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:15.174
I have a daughter who's three
and then a daughter who was born

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:18.244
just two weeks before everything
went into lockdown,

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.645
so she's about to be one now.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:23.149
And then my parents have been
quarantining with us too.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:25.317
So, my mom was out here
when the baby was born,

00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:27.654
and so she's been with us
for a year!

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:28.921
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:33.092
-Which is long, and my father,
who is an attorney

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.027
in New Jersey,
came out in the summer.

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:39.265
So we have just, like,
such a loud house all the time.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.766
It's like my children
are screaming

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:42.769
and I'm in a Zoom room
downstairs,

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.070
and my husband is in a Zoom room
in the baby's room,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:48.608
and my dad's, like, running
a legal practice

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:53.346
in the other kid's room with
a bunch of, like, loud alarms

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:57.483
that, I guess, like, baby
boomers, like guys in their 60s

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.819
just always have a bunch of
different phone alarms

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.153
going off all the time.

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:03.756
My mom is watching, like,
"90 Day Fiancé" at full blast.

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:07.126
It's just, like, not quiet ever.

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:10.329
-I'm always delighted by stories
about your mother.

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:14.100
And she must be spending
a lot of time with your kids.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.901
-Yes, yeah, I know.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:19.172
I made a show, "Great News,"
that was on NBC

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:20.906
and is on Netflix now,
about my mom,

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:24.577
and I'm always so frustrated
because I have stories

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:27.280
every day that are so easy
that I can just steal.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.248
But my mom -- it's really sweet.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:32.118
My mom and my 3-year-old
daughter have, like,

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:35.554
a really sweet relationship,
and they're really close too,

00:02:35.554 --> 00:02:38.757
but over the course of this
year, they've been spending

00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:42.595
so much time together that my
3-year-old, I realize just now,

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.231
has the personality of
a 65-year-old, like,

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:48.701
Italian American woman
from New Jersey, where they,

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:53.372
you know, she's like really
brassy and confident and she --

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:57.910
the other day, my daughter
Celine was, like, on her iPad

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.678
and trying to get, like,
"Paw Patrol"

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:02.548
to load or whatever, and she was
getting frustrated and she goes,

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:04.283
"Oh, [bleep]"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.283 --> 00:03:06.385
And I was like, "What?
What did she say?"

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:10.423
And she was like [bleep],
which I guess is good

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:13.559
that she's not saying
the F-word in English,

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:17.229
but she's just like
Paulie Walnuts or something.

00:03:17.229 --> 00:03:20.400
-I feel like, not to get
too far into the, like,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.668
therapist's office, but I feel
like by naming your daughter

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:26.038
Celine, you tried very hard
not to have that happen.

00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:29.575
-Yes. I wanted -- I wanted
a French fancy person,

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:33.279
but we've just been pulled back
to suburban New Jersey.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:36.615
My mother -- and it's really
sweet because my daughter, like,

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:39.352
loves hearing my mom's stories.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:42.588
Like, my mom is always telling
her about, like, growing up

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:46.926
in the '60s in New Jersey,
and, you know, my daughter is

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:50.429
at that age where she loves to,
like, play things that,

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:54.133
you know, and make us play with
her and, you know.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:57.836
She'll be like, "Let's play
Grandma when she was little,

00:03:57.836 --> 00:04:01.307
and I'll be Grammy
and you be Tommy Tomassi,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:04.643
the guy who owned the meat store
and got Grammy's daddy

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:06.779
tickets to
the fireman's carnival."

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:09.615
-[ Laughs ]
-And I'm like, "I don't know --

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:13.118
I don't know who this guy is,
what his characteristics are,

00:04:13.118 --> 00:04:14.954
what this story is."

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:16.555
-That's fantastic.

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:20.459
Hey, congratulations on this
show, which is really wonderful.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:24.130
And one of the best elements
about it is you are writing

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.731
in the "Saved by the Bell"
universe,

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:29.368
which is something I have heard
that you were doing,

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:30.837
obviously not for television
at the time,

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:32.571
when you were 12 years old.

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:35.341
-Yes, so, you know,
I've been a fan --

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.577
like a lot of people my age,
I've been a fan

00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:40.980
of "Saved by the Bell,"
you know, my whole childhood.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.750
It was just one of those shows
that for some reason was on

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.620
in repeats like 24 hours a day,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.855
on 6 different channels
all the time.

00:04:49.855 --> 00:04:52.458
So it was just a huge part,
and I've heard from other people

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:53.592
in doing the show that it was

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:54.994
a huge part of
their childhood too.

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:57.796
But, you know, my best friend
Renata and I used to make,

00:04:57.796 --> 00:05:00.400
like, funny comedy videos
because we loved "SNL" and stuff

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.903
and we wrote, like, a parody,
like, "Saved by the Bell" movie,

00:05:04.903 --> 00:05:08.074
and when I started -- you know,
when I pitched this show

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:09.742
and started working on it
and stuff,

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:13.112
Renata sent me the script
she still had of it.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.380
And I was like, "Oh, my God,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.115
this is going to be
so embarrassing."

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.483
It was something I wrote
when I was 12.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.052
And when I read it, I was like,

00:05:19.052 --> 00:05:20.452
"You know, it's pretty good.

00:05:20.452 --> 00:05:24.123
It's like 1% better than,
like, the stuff I write now."

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.092
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:30.128
-But you've actually gotten
to work with the original cast.

00:05:30.128 --> 00:05:32.698
That must be just a trip.

00:05:32.698 --> 00:05:34.600
-It's thrilling.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.969
Like, you know,
because I say this

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:39.705
and I feel like people think
I'm stupid or something,

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:42.775
but, like, "Saved by the Bell"
really was huge for me.

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:45.077
It was like my "Star Wars."

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:47.613
That's, like, an embarrassing
thing to admit, I guess.

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:52.351
But, you know, it's so cool
to love something so much

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:54.654
when you're a kid
and then be able to, like,

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:57.122
pitch your own version of it

00:05:57.122 --> 00:05:59.058
that is very much done
in reverence

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.960
but also a little bit
poking fun at it.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.363
And it has created this
situation where there's, like,

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:08.067
you know, the 10-year-old
inside me is getting --

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:12.471
has too much power and is like
a child tyrant running the show.

00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:16.642
Like, you know,
I loved Zack and Kelly so much,

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:20.612
and, like, they showed me
what love is meant to be.

00:06:20.612 --> 00:06:22.582
-[ Laughs ]
-And, you know, I was, like,

00:06:22.582 --> 00:06:27.253
in situations this year where
I'm, like, producing a scene

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:30.056
where I can be like,
"Kiss again, Zack and Kelly.

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:33.859
Do it again. Kiss her longer,"
or whatever.

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:36.161
And I'm like, "What are you
doing? Be an adult."

00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:38.598
-Well, I will say,
I think it's a show --

00:06:38.598 --> 00:06:41.400
I know it to be a show that a
lot of people care deeply about.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.469
And it is in such a safe pair of
hands with you.

00:06:44.469 --> 00:06:46.038
Congratulations on it,

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:47.840
and thanks so much
for spending time with us.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.875
-Thanks so much. This was fun.

00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:52.578
-Season 1 of "Saved by the Bell"
is streaming now on Peacock.

